Date: Thu, 24 Jun 1993 05:05:49 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Self-Reproduction, Complexity, and the Food Chain

Derek Zahn and Tim May make good comments on self-rep machines.
In turn, I have the following thoughts and questions:

Would outside info slow down self-rep process for von Neumann
machines?  Light travels faster than the probes.  By beaming out
updates and fixes, and "printing them out" on the VNM's 3d printers,
mills, robot arms, etc. the replication rate might be increased
well beyond the original design spec.  For Drexler's nanometer-scale
machines information transmission might start to be costly (in
entropic heat production) compared with less flexible local storage 
of control information.  

Machines which use large amounts of outside information are likely
key to opening up physical frontiers.  Blacksmith's forges
relied on the skill of the blacksmith to create a wide variety of
farm implements.  For large-scale space industries such flexibility
will likely be combinined with automation, eg robot arms and 3D
printers.

I don't have the numbers handy, but some clays can have quite
complex patterns of aluminum-for-silicon substitution, overlapping
the complexity of some simple enzymes.  This, and the fact that
there is some degree of mutual recognition and catalysis between
certain clays and enzymes, gives rise to Cairn-Smiths theory
on the clay origin of life.  Indeed if we knew how life originated,
that would tell us how hard it might be to create self-reproduction 
of metabolism and structure de novo.  The same data structure,
the auto-processing building block graph, is likely key to both areas.  
Cf. my earlier post "Drexler Assemblers and the Origin of Life".

The algorithmic complexity of a 747 seems almost as unclear as
that for a hypothetical VNM.  Where does the 250 MB come from?
The size of CAD files?  But some of that info is redundant;
some is just pointers to subcontractor's parts, and much of the info
needed to make a part lies outside the CAD file, eg in the mind of
lathe operators.  Here's an example closer to home -- what
is the algorithmic complexity of the Pentium?  Do we count 
complexity generated by silicon compilers; why or why not?

I've seen dozen-person software projects generate more source
code than the 4 MB in E. Coli's genome.  I've single-handedly ported 
programs that large (not making anywhere near MB's worth of changes,
to be sure).  The design space is similary large;
is there good reason to believe that E. Coli's 4 MB
of genome is vastly more complex than 4 MB of source code?
A Turing-equivalent source code interpreter
is more flexible than the protein assembly process, so 
the software engineers can algorithmically compress their design
into fewer MB of instructions than could the evolution of E. Coli.
On the other hand, there is quite a bit going on in the "DNA intepreter"
that we don't understand very well (esp. the folding, enzymatic
activity, etc. of proteins), and the software interpreter 
(including the computer it runs on) is itself quite a bit more
complex than 4 MB.  "Standing on the shoulders of giants".

Derek Zahn observes the importance of creating formalisms for
the physical characteristics of the parts and processes, in order
to automate design.  The most important property for assembly seems
to be geometry.  Some CAD programs implement a neat formal language to 
design 3D shapes as a tree of operators (union, intersection, difference)
over solid primitives (ellipsoids, cuboids, spheroids, splinoids,
cones, cylinders).  The shape of nearly all parts can be defined this 
way, although some aerodynamic parts are tough.  Allosteric 
properties -- the way shapes interact with each other during assembly --
can also be modelled with this kind of language, and it looks ideal for
automated design via GP.  On my "to do" list is to look at the
Autolisp shape-definition data structures & operators and, if
suitable, port GP to Autolisp and implement some fitness functions
based on shape.  (Autolisp GP might be a good idea just on
its own, at that).

Formalisms for material properties may be tougher.  One strategy is
to pick a small number of very flexible materials (eg Drexler 
picks diamandoid).  I'm concerned about error propagation
as a function of materials properties (see extruder example below).

My divide-and-conquer strategy is to break down the self-rep problem into 
self-assembly (eg the "hardware store" factory), self-shaping (eg 
extruding, milling, parts for another factory, but not assembling them),
self-processing (processing chemicals into the materials needed
to build more plants), and self-mining (mining all the raw
materials needed to create the mining equipment, but not
processing, shaping, or assembling them), etc.  Those subtasks
alone are often quite challenging, and there remains the challenge
of fusing steps so that the mine extracts materials for the 
entire system, ..., the assembler assembles all the parts for
the entire system.

Tim May suggests a self-assembling factory built from
hardware-store parts as a preliminary challenge, and Derek
Zahn suggests substituting plastic for metal where possible.
An even simpler venue for studying self-rep might be toy sets like 
Lego Technic, which is used to build mobile robots for research 
and education.  Giving in to the little boy within, :-)  I've actually 
built some rod-logic gates (sans springs), a lego "tape" and "tape reader", 
and a rod-driven brick joiner out of legos.  As with real assembly, the 
flexibility of the joiner is somewhat limited, but on the other hand I 
don't need to make all possible combinations to build the joiner, tape, 
and rod logic gates.  There also might be problems with applying 
sufficient force to the joiner with the Technic electric motor instead of 
cheating by driving the joiner rod by hand.  If successful, I should be 
able to put Lego Rabbit in a gym full of loose lego bricks, electric motors,
and perhaps sensors and microcircuits, and the room should end up
full of Lego Rabbits.

I'd also like to model the solid geometry of legos
in Autocad, and automatically generate the closed-loop assembly
graph and geometry for Lego Rabbit with GP/Autolisp. 

Unabashed, I've also played with the Playdoh Fun Factory, which 
extrudes various kinds of beams through variously shaped plastic
dies.  I've designed a geometry for extruding walls and stamping
more dies, which would be a self-shaping factory.  Alas, there will
likely be a major problem with error amplification across
the generations, until the dies are grossly mishapen, the walls
don't fit together, etc.  Similar problems extend to shaping
little clay lego bricks, and the more fancy motors, censors,
circuits, etc. in the robot kit are out of the question for
this toy extruder.

It may be debatable whether these toy systems
are closer to my self-replicating furnace (forest fire :-)
or a useful self-replicating factory.  They're certainly 
intended to approach the latter, but in terms of producing
products that beat the quality of Japanese imports, probably
not. :-)

Tim May:
> Drexler estimates (as Merkle would say at this point: "Wave
> hands...grumble...wave hands...") that any gadget about the size of a
> breadbox (a computer, another factory, etc.) could then be built for a few
> dollars worth of sugar solution input and electricity.

Drexler's argument is based on the frequency of assembling the basic
diamondoid parts from simple carbon molecules, and the frequency of
higher subassembly steps.  The big win comes from the high
frequency of the most primitive eutactic operation of assembling
the basic diamondoid part.  (Here's an opportunity to delve
into _Nanosystems_ if somebody wants to argue for or against
Drexler's numbers; Table 14.1 on page 422.  I'm agnostic so far,
because I haven't looked looked into all the details of the eutactic 
mechanosynthesis of diamond, whether he can achieve that at 1 Mhz).


