In this post I'll just deal with the "information content" aspects of Nick
Szabo's excellent comments. The Lego/factory/self-replicator ideas will be
the subject of a separate posting.

Nick Szabo writes:

>The algorithmic complexity of a 747 seems almost as unclear as
>that for a hypothetical VNM.  Where does the 250 MB come from?
>The size of CAD files?  But some of that info is redundant;
>some is just pointers to subcontractor's parts, and much of the info
>needed to make a part lies outside the CAD file, eg in the mind of
>lathe operators.  Here's an example closer to home -- what
>is the algorithmic complexity of the Pentium?  Do we count 
>complexity generated by silicon compilers; why or why not?

Agreed, the estimate is very crude, and depends on a lot of "assumed
knowledge" or other parts, other databases, nouns, verbs, etc. This is
common in complex economies/ecologies/"repro-ologies" (a  neoligism).

A von Neumann probe, or Drexlerian asteroid-mining self-replicator, must
either carry this ancillary information with it (and I'm making no claims
that this will be impossible) or must simplify/compress in some clever way.


>
>I've seen dozen-person software projects generate more source
>code than the 4 MB in E. Coli's genome.  I've single-handedly ported 
>programs that large (not making anywhere near MB's worth of changes,
>to be sure).  The design space is similary large;
>is there good reason to believe that E. Coli's 4 MB
>of genome is vastly more complex than 4 MB of source code?
>A Turing-equivalent source code interpreter
>is more flexible than the protein assembly process, so 
>the software engineers can algorithmically compress their design
>into fewer MB of instructions than could the evolution of E. Coli.
>On the other hand, there is quite a bit going on in the "DNA intepreter"
>that we don't understand very well (esp. the folding, enzymatic
>activity, etc. of proteins), and the software interpreter 
>(including the computer it runs on) is itself quite a bit more
>complex than 4 MB.  "Standing on the shoulders of giants".

I don't know for sure, of course, but I suspect the "4 MB" of e. coli is
enormous complex. In my second post to the Extropians list, last summer's
"Self-Reproduction is Hard," I speculated that the "hackerish" code, with
lots of point mutations, rentrant points, etc., probably implies a very
large algorithmic information content (~ complexity). The Chaitin articles
on biology, evolution, and complexity make similar points. (I posted them
earlier today...we can debate them in our AIT VirtSem.)

There are a lot of "tricks" that nature uses for cell operation and cell
division that we are only now beginning to understand, things like the 2D
constraints imposed by lipid membranes, which appear to play an important
role in positioning things inside the cell. (I'm not a cell biologist, and
I just picked this up from some discussions with others who have more
expertise. Many believe that evolution has made great use of lots of
"obscure" tricks....how many of these remain for us to "discover" (in the
sense of reductionistic knowledge of these mechanisms) is an open question.
My guess: a lot.)

Other points in Nick's post I'll deal with in a separate post.

-Tim
