Well, long before solving the _materials processing_ aspects, which I agree
is interesting if one's interests and background are in this area (mine
aren't, and my device physics background had very little to do with lathes,
mills, extrusion of gears, and space-based vacuum processing!), one needs
to look at the very basic problems of self-reproduction. That is, I favor
not getting bogged down in discussions of iron ore processing on the moon,
refining of nickel in asteroids, and the like. (A legitimate area, to be
sure.)

>
>Tim May suggests a self-assembling factory built from
>hardware-store parts as a preliminary challenge, and Derek

Indeed, this is the "challenge" I have levelled at "strong nanotech"
supporters for several years now, especially at the Palo Alto-based
nanotech discussion group ("Assembler Multitude").

Invocation of submicron-sized components will not solve the problem of
*what to do with the widgets*.  The problem is not trivial. 

>Zahn suggests substituting plastic for metal where possible.
>An even simpler venue for studying self-rep might be toy sets like 
>Lego Technic, which is used to build mobile robots for research 
>and education.  Giving in to the little boy within, :-)  I've actually 
>built some rod-logic gates (sans springs), a lego "tape" and "tape reader", 
>and a rod-driven brick joiner out of legos.  As with real assembly, the 
>flexibility of the joiner is somewhat limited, but on the other hand I 
>don't need to make all possible combinations to build the joiner, tape, 
>and rod logic gates.  There also might be problems with applying 
>sufficient force to the joiner with the Technic electric motor instead of 
>cheating by driving the joiner rod by hand.  If successful, I should be 
>able to put Lego Rabbit in a gym full of loose lego bricks, electric motors,
>and perhaps sensors and microcircuits, and the room should end up
>full of Lego Rabbits.
>
>I'd also like to model the solid geometry of legos
>in Autocad, and automatically generate the closed-loop assembly
>graph and geometry for Lego Rabbit with GP/Autolisp. 

Let me encourage Nick in the strongest possible way!!! To build a gadget
that can roam through a space of fairly low-level components (Lego blocks,
Tinkertoys, the rods and gears of the "hardware store" model I often use)
and build more such gadgets that can then do the same thing, would be a
stunning achievement.

Chris Langton, of A-LIFE fame, has attempted this, and settled on the
abstract automata route that von Neumann and others were forced into
following. ("Forced" in the sense of making no real progress toward 3D,
real-space automata).

Provided there is no "cheating," such as the assembly of high-level,
pre-built components (like the halves of trains example), such an
achievement would ensure fame and a position for life at the Santa Fe
Institute.

The difficulties are many, even with external control software (a major
simplifying factor, but one which most nanotech enthusiasts, and me, are
prepared to grant). The Lego blocks must make the motors (how?), the local
logic (get ready for using a _lot_ of blocks!), mus be able to find the
"food"

<<Interrruption: supplying the Lego blocks by automated conveyer belts at
just the right time is a "solution" that occurs to many. This is cheating!
If such tricks were to be allowed, then a car factory would be called
"self-reproducing." It is important that the "food" be low-level and
randomly distributed. (Of course, a large factory which built another large
factory would meet the terms of the challenge. :-} ) >>

and then use the "food" to build the parts it needs to make more gadgets.

I repeat: this is a very difficult problem!

Like I said, the fundamental issues are not really about how to get nickel
ore out of asteroids, or iron out of mines, or whatnot. The fundamental
issues can be dealt with "abstractly" (but no so abstractly that the
self-replication is occuring at the level of cellular automata, or
s-expressions, or the like), with fairly idealized raw materials. (Once we
have some basic idea of how a physical, 3D-space machine can make more of
itself, we can worry in earnest about friction, flaws, sizing, etc.)

I can scan and OCR some good material on the von Neumann/Codd/Smith/etc.
approaches to self-reproducing automata, if there's interest.

-Tim May

