beside the point.)
 
>Art with enough "guts" to merit discussion works magic on the psyche,
>focuses the inner vision to symbols and patterns invoked by the
>artist. For art to _be_ (that word again) purely subjective as you
>say, it must also be quite shallow -- or maybe just so incoherent that
>it's a Rohrshach test.
 
Again, I wish I were able to explain myself better. As far as I can
tell, I mostly agree with you. The PHYSICAL EXISTENCE of the art is
objective; the MEANING intended by the artist and the INTERPRETATION
performed by the recipient are subjective. "Art" is not a physical,
objective thing; rather, it denotes a state of mind. As Frank Zappa
said, for something to be art, you merely have to:
 
 1) Declare your intention to create a composition.
 2) Start a piece at some time.
 3) Cause something to happen over a period of time (it doesn't
    matter what happens in your time hole -- we have critics to
    tell us whether it's any good or not, so we won't worry about
    that part).
 4) End the piece at some time (or keep it going, telling the
    audience it is a 'work in progress').
 5) Get a part-time job so you can continue to do stuff like this.
 
 [From _The Real Frank Zappa Book_, 1990]
 
 
>Rock music, for example, invokes the libido, provoking the listener to
>confront his (and especially her :-) "wild side". Having done this, it
>can either leave you there (fine for party, lame for arty), or bring
>in other symbols or archetypes to lead you to make new connections. If
>this is done too heavy-handedly, you get preachy stuff (like a lot of
>Rush) that is easily resented and rejected. Folk music similarly
>invokes the shared past to predispose the listener to accept the
>message of the song as if it came from his ancestors.
 
I dunno; maybe my prime bitch is just because I believe art isn't
something that other people can define. Only you can decide for yourself
whether it's art; only you can decide whether you like it, and you may
have reasons for liking it, and you may not. Sort of like the (tolerant)
Christian view that "All must come to Christ of their own free will",
and Objectivism's view that "Everyone must interpret reality on their
own."
 
|> I had some rather nasty e-mail arguments with
|> people who claimed that by examining my tastes in music, literature,
|> etc., they could determine whether or not I was an Objectivist.. the
|> proper reply to which, of course, is, "Screw you and the horse you
|> rode in on."
 
>No, that's a flip reply, not a proper one.
 
Well, I guess it depends on what your definition of "proper" is. For
these idiots, it most certainly was the proper reply.
 
>I translate what you were challenged to do as "provide evidence that
>your belief in and committment to Objectivism has sufficient depth
>to guide your aesthetic choices".
 
In a nutshell, yes. And I contend that:
 
 1) Whether Objectivism is valid or not is up to me to decide, and
 2) Whether I believe the essential tenets of Objectivism or not is
    up to me, and me alone, to determine, and
 3) Whether or not I hold the tenets of Objectivism as valid has
    absolutely nothing to do with what my personal likes and dislikes
    are; it merely means that I do not use force against others except
    in self-defense, and as far as anyone else but myself is concerned,
    that's all that matters, because
 4) I don't allow Objectivism to determine my aesthetic choices.
 
 
>Now, you may refuse to submit yourself to such examination, or you may
>dispute the validity of the [O*]bjectivist analysis...but why dodge the
>question like this? I don't approve of their cultish attitude, either,
>and hereby hangs the point that Tim made: you don't _want_ a single
>comfortable philosophy that you can use to make immutable choices that
>never need revisiting. I enjoy the freedom to try on viewpoints -- but
>this requires understanding them.
 
It really wasn't an attempt to dodge the question; it was (IMO) a valid
response, because as far as I'm concerned, philosophy has nothing to do
with art. It isn't the "cultish attitude" that pisses me off, because I
don't consider irrational (cultish) people to be Objectivists. Rather,
it's the notion that Objectivism has the answers not only to rational
questions such as the nature of man and the proper role of government,
but also to such silliness as "What sort of music should I like?" and
"What sort of painting expresses a 'sense of life'?".
 
I've used Objectivism to make immutable choices -- but these choices
are based on objective reality and the nature of man. I don't use it to
determine my views or attitudes on things that are not properly in the
realm of philosophy.
 
>I prefer to "enjoy first, analyze later" rather than "enjoy first,
>postpone analysis indefinitely". Of course, some things cry out for
>analysis in real-time, such as visual art that uses obvious symbolism.
 
And sometimes, even after I analyze something, even if I determine for
myself that it's expressing some sort of message (by my interpretation)
that I find personally distasteful, I still enjoy experiencing it. Quite
a lot of music falls into this category -- a song may have lyrics which
glorify collectivism and sacrifice, but that doesn't determine whether
or not I enjoy listening to it. Actually, now that I think about it, I
believe music is the only thing that falls into this dual category,
since it has both the rational (lyrics) and emotional (music) components.
If I don't like the lyrics, I may still enjoy experiencing the song, but
if I don't like the music, I definitely won't enjoy experiencing it, no
matter how much I admire the lyrics. Paintings, sculptures, etc., all
have one element in them -- the emotional -- unless you're talking about
Andy Warhol's things that are plastered with words, which (IMO) detracts
from any message which might be there, and is usually used to cover up
the fact that there isn't any message there in the first place.
 
Your response was very much appreciated; as you can see, it helped me
clarify my thoughts on the subject a little better. Further discussion
is welcomed (we can always take it to mail if it degenerates into one
of these Romana-spanking things... ;)
 
 
 


