Date: Thu, 24 Jun 93 23:41:39 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: "Algorithmic Information and Evolution"

I appreciate Tim scanning in Chaitin's article, but I have to say that
I really don't follow the proposed connection between AIT and evolution.

Chaitin's Omega analogy shows that if you compute for an infinite amount
of time you can generate infinite complexity.  But his example doesn't
relate at all to the fundamental idea of evolution, the role of natural
selection applied to self-replicating systems.  There is no assurance
whatsoever that evolution will lead to infinite complexity.  We might
hope that it will, but I don't see how Chaitin's argument sheds any light.

Chaitin also points out that biological evolution can be thought of as
having done a lot of computation.  Perhaps so, but then, so can the planets
in their orbits around the sun, as well as the waves striking the beach.
Again, there is no guarantee that the computation is useful or productive
in terms of generating new complexity.

Chaitin warns that his point is abstract, and perhaps I am being too
literal in trying to see an analogy between AIT and evolution.

The one place I can see a possible connection between AIT and evolution
would be by analogy to the AIT result that a formal system can't prove
theorems more complex than the size of its axioms.  Perhaps this could
be taken to suggest that an evolutionary process is similarly limited
in terms of the complexity that it can produce?

Hal Finney

